        "text_en": "The descending Dhamanis carry downward Vata, urine, faeces, semen, menstrual blood, and the like. On reaching the Pittashaya, they release the essence of food and drink digested there by the action of heat, distribute it through the body, nourishing it, and also pass a portion of it to the ascending and transverse Dhamanis, filling the reservoir of Rasa, and they discharge urine, faeces, and sweat. Between the receptacle stomach and the stomach of digestion, they divide into three each, making thirty branches in all. Of these, ten, in pairs, carry Vata, Pitta, Kapha, blood, and Rasa; two, connected with the intestines, carry food; two carry water; two, leading to the bladder, carry urine; two carry semen, effecting its formation, and another two its discharge; these same two in women carry menstrual blood; two, connected with the large intestine, expel faeces; and the remaining eight, among the transverse Dhamanis, distribute sweat. Thus these thirty branches have been described in detail. Through them everything below the navel is sustained: the stomach of digestion, the loins, the bladder, the faeces, the anus, the pelvic region, the penis, and the legs."
