        "text_en": "Mahasugandhi agada: The following substances, namely: (red) sandalwood, aguru, kushtha, tagara, tilaparnika, prapaundarika, nalada, sarala, devadaru, bhadrashri (white sandalwood), yavaphala, bhargi, nili, sugandhika, kaleyaka, padmaka, madhuka, nagara, jata (a kind of jatamamsi), punnaga, ela, elavalu, gairika, dhyanaka, bala, toya, sarjarasa, mamsi, sitapushpa, harenuka, talishapatra, small ela, priyangu, kutannata, shailapushpa, shaileya, patra, kalanu-sariva, trikatu, shitashiva (Dallana explains that 'shitashiva' means camphor, while other commentators hold it to be shami), kasmarya, katurohini, somaraji, ativisha, prithvika, indravaruni, ushira, varuna, musta, nakha, kustumburu, both kinds of shveta (the text has 'shveta' in the dual, meaning white vacha and white aparajita; Dallana gives only one), haridra, sthaunaya, laksha, the five kinds of medicinal salt, kumuda, utpala, padma, the flower of arka, the flowers and fruit of champaka, ashoka, sumanas, tilaka (sesame), patali, shalmali, shelu, shirisha, surasa, trinashuli, and sindhuvara, the flowers of dhava, ashvakarna, and tinisha, guggulu, kumkuma, vimbi, sarpakshi, and gandha-nakuli — should be carefully gathered and ground with honey, ghee, and cow's bile, and then kept in a horn (or a receptacle made of that material). This remedy, the best of all antidotal preparations, is able to snatch from the jaws of death a patient even with drooping shoulders and sunken, rolled-back eyes. It is capable of instantly destroying the irresistible fiery venom even of the fearsome, enraged Vasuki, king of serpents. This agada, consisting of eighty-five ingredients, is called Mahasugandhi agada and is the most powerful of all antidotal remedies. It should be kept constantly with the king. Anointed with this compound, he will surely win the love of all his subjects and will shine with his royal majesty even among his enemies."
