        "text_en": "Remedies for various cases: An antidotal compound (agada) containing kushtha, chakra (tagasi), vacha, roots of vilva, patha, suvarchika, household soot, and both kinds of haridra is effective for the bite of the Trikantaka insect. An agada containing household soot, kirata, chakra, kushtha, and the seeds of palasha destroys the venom of the Galagoli insect. An agada consisting of kumkuma, tagara, shigru, padmaka, and two kinds of rajani, ground with water, is curative for the bite of the shatapadi (centipede). An agada consisting of meshashringi, vacha, patha, nichula, rohini, and balaka is effective in all kinds of poisoning by the manduka (frog). An agada consisting of vacha, ashvagandha, atibala, bala, atiguha (shalaparni), and agula (prishniparni) neutralizes the venom of the Vishvambhara insect. An agada consisting of shirisha, tagara, kushtha, two kinds of haridra, amshumati, and two kinds of sahi destroys the venom of the Ahinduka insect. For the bite of Kandumaka, cooling measures should be applied at night, since the venom, aggravated by the sun's rays during the day, does not respond to any remedy applied at that time. An agada consisting of chakra, kushtha, and apamarga is effective for the bite of Shukavrinta. As an alternative, earth from a black anthill, ground with the pressed juice of bhringi, will prove effective in such cases. A plaster prepared from black anthill earth and cow's urine is curative for the bites of flies, ants, and mosquitoes. The treatment for the bite of Pratisuryaka is the same as for snakebite."
