        "text_en": "The appearance of dark-brown spots on the sclera (shukla) of the eye, resembling flesh in color or having the coloration of an oyster shell, is called shuktika. The appearance of a single point or spot on the sclera (shukla), colored like a drop of hare's blood, is called arjuna. A convex, round spot on the white coat, white in color, resembling mashed rice and transparent as water, is called pishtaka. Red, extensive areas of hardened veins spreading over the white coat and looking like a network are called sira-jala. Numerous white pustule-like growths (pidaka) on the sclera (shukla), near the border with the black coat (the iris), covered with fragments of veins, are called sira-pidaka. The disease in which a spot the color of Indian bell-bronze (kamsya), covered by a vein (sira), appears in the area of the sclera, is called balasa."
